course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 22
title: Farm animals, forest animals and pets
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In summer Sashko visits his granny's village, where there's a korOva (cow), a svynya (pig), a kurka (hen) and a pIven (rooster) shouting at dawn, and a leleka, a stork, nesting on a tall pole or roof.
explain: The forest animals come from fairy tales: vovk the wolf, vedmId the bear, zayets the hare and lysYtsya the fox, like the sly "Lys Mykyta".
explain: Careful: animal names can be insults. Calling a person "korOva", "svynya" or "zhAba" is rude — but "zaichyk", little hare, and "rybka", little fish, are loving names for small children.
explain: To ask about a friend's pet: "u tebe ye kit?" — literally "at you is a cat?", do you have a cat? — and then "yak yoho zvut?", what's his name?
fact: "korOva" means cow; calling a person "korOva" is an insult.
fact: "svynya" means pig; calling someone "svynya" is an insult meaning a pig or slob.
fact: "kurka" means hen, and "pIven" means rooster.
fact: "zayets" means hare or bunny, and "zaichyk", little hare, is a loving name for a small child.
fact: "vedmId" means bear, and "vovk" means wolf; both appear in many fairy tales.
fact: "lysYtsya" means fox; the sly fox "Lys Mykyta" is a famous Ukrainian tale character.
fact: "ryba" means fish, and "rybka", little fish, is a loving name too.
fact: "zhAba" means frog or toad; as an insult it means an ugly or greedy person, and "zhAba davyt", "the toad squeezes", means someone is greedy or envious.
fact: "leleka" means stork; storks nest on village roofs and poles in Ukraine and are a symbol of home.
fact: In old stories storks bring luck and babies.
fact: "u tebe ye kit?" means "do you have a cat?", literally "at you is a cat?", and the answer is "tak" or "ni".
fact: "yak yoho zvut?" means "what's his name?", used when meeting a pet.
exercise: At granny's village Sashko milks a "korOva". What animal is it? | A cow | A goat | A sheep | A pig
exercise: Why should you never call a person "korOva"? | It is an insult | It is a word only for babies | It means "teacher" | It is a holy word
exercise: What is a "svynya"? | A pig | A swan | A sheep | A goose
exercise: What are "kurka" and "pIven"? | A hen and a rooster | A duck and a drake | A goose and a gander | A sparrow and a crow
exercise: In the story the "zayets" runs very fast with long ears. What is it? | A hare | A squirrel | A mouse | A deer
exercise: Sashko's mum calls her toddler "zaichyk". Is she being mean? | No, it's a loving name: little hare | Yes, it means slowcoach, a slow child | Yes, it means scaredy-cat, a coward | No, it's the word for a little puppy
exercise: Which animal is a "vedmId"? | A bear | A badger | A wolf | A boar
exercise: The big bad "vovk" in the fairy tale is which animal? | A wolf | A fox | A bear | A dog
exercise: Who is "Lys Mykyta" in Ukrainian tales? | A sly fox | A brave wolf | A wise owl | A clever hare
exercise: Sashko feeds his "rybky" in the tank. What does he keep? | Little fish | Little frogs | Little birds | Little mice
exercise: Sashko catches a "zhAba" at the pond. What is it? | A frog or toad | A fish | A snail | A duck
exercise: A big kid calls a girl "zhAba". What is he doing? | Insulting her, calling her ugly or greedy | Saying she swims well | Calling her a sweetheart, a loving little pet | Saying she sings well
exercise: What is a "leleka"? | A stork | A swallow | A heron | A seagull
exercise: You want to know if Sashko's cousin has a cat. What do you ask? | u tebe ye kit? | yak teBE zvut? | de ty zhyvEsh? | SKILky tobI rokIv?
exercise: Sashko shows you his new puppy. How do you ask its name? | yak yoho zvut? | yak spRAvy? | shcho stAlosya? | de tualEt?
test: my mate says Ukrainians are rude cos they call kids zaichyk. right? | No, zaichyk is a loving name | Yes, it means stupid, a rude word | Yes, it means "little pig" | No, it means puppy
test: Sashko's granny says "zhAba davyt" about a neighbour who won't share apples. What does she mean? | He's greedy | He's ill | He's scared of frogs | He's very old
test: In a cartoon, someone answers "u tebe ye kit?" with "MEne zvut Murchyk". What went wrong? | He gave a name instead of yes or no | Nothing, that's the right answer | He should say "DObryi DEN" | He should say "zhakh!"
test: Why might a Ukrainian village house have a big nest on a pole near the roof? | A stork nests there, a symbol of home | It's a bird trap, to catch birds for dinner | Owls are kept for mice | It's a decoration made of sticks
test: Sashko's brother yells "svynya!" at him for eating all the sweets. Is it a compliment? | No, an insult: pig, slob | Yes, it means "clever" | Yes, it means "fast eater" | No, it means "hungry"
test: Granny says the leleka brought the baby. What is she telling? | An old story about storks | A real fact about storks | A joke about wolves | A rule about hens
test: "u tebe ye kit?" — why does it sound odd in English? | It is literally "at you is a cat?" | It is literally "is a cat you?" | It is literally "cat at me?" | It is a word-for-word English phrase
test: A sly character tricks everyone in Sashko's storybook. Which animal is it most likely? | lysYtsya, the fox | korOva, the cow | kurka, the hen | ryba, the fish
test: Sashko's dad calls his little sister "rybko". Is he cross? | No, it's a loving name: little fish | Yes, it means "slimy", like a cold fish | Yes, it means "wet" | No, it means "kitten"
test: At dawn something on the farm shouts very loudly. Which animal? | pIven, the rooster | kurka, the hen | korOva, the cow | zayets, the hare
test: What would happen if you called Sashko's mum "korOva" as a joke? | She'd be offended: it's an insult | She'd laugh: it means "pretty" | She'd thank you | Nothing, it means "lady"
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the use of the nouns "korOva", "svynya" and "zhAba" when applied to persons? | Such usage constitutes an insult | Such usage constitutes a term of endearment | Such usage is reserved for formal occasions | Such usage is a respectful mode of address
exam: Identify the bird which, in Ukrainian tradition, nests upon village roofs and poles and is regarded as a symbol of home. | The stork, "leleka" | The rooster, "pIven" | The hen, "kurka" | The swallow, "lastivka"
exam: It is given that a child wishes to ascertain whether an interlocutor possesses a cat. Select the appropriate enquiry. | u tebe ye kit? | yak yoho zvut? | yak teBE zvut? | de ty zhyvEsh?
exam: Ascertain the significance of the diminutive "zaichyk" when employed by a mother towards a small child. | It is an expression of affection | It is an accusation of cowardice | It is a reproach for slowness | It is an instruction to be silent
